{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2131201979/11/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"e12f9a14\" lines=\"138-152\">\n<description>\nEmit diagonal beams from framed 2x2 blocks through frame gaps; beams interact on collision.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Wall color (e.g. green) forms 4x4 frames around 2x2 monochrome blocks.\n- Each frame has one or more gap pixels where the wall is missing.\n\nRule:\n- Each gap emits a beam of the inner block's color, traveling diagonally outward (direction set by gap position relative to the block).\n- Beams paint every cell they cross until they hit the grid edge or collide.\n- 90° collision: both beams turn 45° toward their bisector direction; the intersection cell is left blank (1-pixel gap).\n- 45° collision: beams merge into a zigzag (alternating cardinal + diagonal steps) with no gap at the meeting point.\n\nKey insight: Two distinct collision rules — 90° produces a gap and single-pixel diagonal lines; 45° produces no gap and thicker zigzag bands.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2d0172a1\" lines=\"209-224\">\n<description>\nConvert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground 4-connected objects are rough closed frames or small filled blobs.\n- Frames may contain other frames/blobs; blobs outside a frame remain outside it.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the containment tree of foreground objects using holes of each frame object.\n- Replace every blob with one foreground pixel.\n- Replace every frame with a rectangular border padded two cells around its direct children.\n- Place siblings by their original row/column overlap and order, with one empty cell between canonical objects.\n\nKey insight: The exact wobbly geometry is irrelevant; only enclosure plus relative sibling positions define the output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"b745798f\" lines=\"18-33\">\n<description>\nExpand each 3-pixel L marker into a same-color corner L on an empty background.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid is an odd square with one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground object is a monochromatic 4-connected 3-pixel L inside a\n  2x2 box.\n\nRule\n- Clear the grid to the background.\n- For each marker, map its L orientation to the corresponding outer corner\n  and draw that corner L in the marker color with arm length half the grid.\n\nKey insight\n- The small marker is a miniature corner; its orientation, not its position,\n  chooses which outer corner receives the expanded L.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"833966f4\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nSwap the upper two-pixel segment and the lower two-pixel segment around the fixed center pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is a 5x1 vertical column of individual colored pixels.\n- The middle pixel is a separator between two same-size two-pixel segments.\n\nRule:\n- Keep the middle pixel in place.\n- Reverse the order of the two pixels above the middle.\n- Reverse the order of the two pixels below the middle.\n\nKey insight:\n- The transformation is local vertical order reversal on each side of the center, not a color-specific rule.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8698868d\" lines=\"9-23\">\n<description>\nMatch sprites to frames by connected-hole count, then nest each sprite inside its frame.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = azure (8). Large hollow squares (frames) arranged in a grid; smaller filled squares (sprites) placed elsewhere.\n- Each frame and sprite has a distinct color. Sprites may contain holes (non-sprite-color pixels).\n\nRule:\n- Frames form a regular grid; some may be bg-colored (invisible).\n- Count connected hole regions inside each frame's interior and each sprite.\n- Pair each frame with the sprite sharing the same hole count.\n- Place the matched sprite inside the frame border; recolor sprite holes to the frame's color.\n\nKey insight: The matching key is the number of connected hole components, not hole area or color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"9aaea919\" lines=\"38-51\">\n<description>\nTransfer sprites from red-marked lanes to green-marked lanes, recoloring and stacking.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Red (2) and green (3) thin bars define vertical lanes.\n- Each lane contains repeated identical monochrome sprites in an evenly spaced vertical grid.\n\nRule:\n- Each red lane's sprites turn grey (5); their count transfers to the nearest green lane.\n- New sprites fill empty lattice slots bottom-up, using the source shape recolored to the destination lane's sprite color.\n- All marker bars are removed.\n\nKey insight: Sprite count is conserved across lanes — red sprites become grey, and equally many new sprites stack into the green lane's regular grid.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"522fdd07\" lines=\"8-23\">\n<description>\nCycle each solid odd-sided square to the next smaller centered square, wrapping a single pixel to a 9x9 square.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Every foreground object is a separated 4-connected monochrome solid square.\n- Square side lengths are odd and belong to the cycle 1, 3, 5, 7, 9.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background.\n- Keep each square's color and center.\n- Replace side length n by n - 2 when n > 1; replace side length 1 by side length 9.\n\nKey insight:\n- The visible operation is on square size, not color: side lengths cycle 1 -> 9 -> 7 -> 5 -> 3 -> 1.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"136b0064\" lines=\"25-39\">\n<description>\nDraw a polyline by decoding 3x3 glyph instructions read across a divided grid.\n\nInput structure:\n- Left of a full-height vertical divider: 3x3 colored glyphs on bg.\n- Right of divider: a single gray (5) start pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Remove the divider; shift the gray start into the left canvas.\n- Read glyphs in column-major order (left→right, top→bottom).\n- Each glyph's shape (pixel area) encodes a segment: area 4 → down 2, area 5 → right 3, area 6 → left 4, area 7 → left 2.\n- Append segments sequentially, stepping down one cell between each.\n\nKey insight: Glyph pixel count (area) uniquely determines both segment length and direction — the shape itself is the instruction.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"c4d1a9ae\" lines=\"28-48\">\n<description>\nFill each vertical foreground band's background cells with the color of the next band to its right.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Each other color occupies a separate vertical band defined by that color's\n  minimum and maximum columns.\n- Within a band, all cells are either that band's color or background.\n\nRule\n- Order the bands from left to right.\n- In each band, replace background pixels with the color of the next band in\n  that order, wrapping from the rightmost band back to the leftmost.\n- Preserve all existing foreground pixels and all background pixels outside\n  the bands.\n\nKey insight\n- The column bands form a cyclic palette legend: a band's gaps are completed\n  by borrowing the color from its right-hand neighbor, not by changing the\n  band's existing pixels.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"64efde09\" lines=\"68-83\">\n<description>\nCopy colored control-ray ports from one rectangular sprite orientation to every matching transformed sprite.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg is the most common color.\n- Several dense 2-by-N or N-by-2 rectangular sprites are dihedral transforms of one template.\n- Isolated single-pixel control markers sit on rays projected outward from the dominant long edge of some sprites.\n\nRule:\n- For each sprite, the active edge is the long side whose cells have the strongest same-color majority.\n- A control marker seen first along an outward ray from an active-edge cell assigns its color to that template cell.\n- On every transformed sprite, draw that assigned color outward from the corresponding active-edge cell through bg cells until blocked or out of bounds.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marker positions are not absolute; they label cells of a shared rectangular template, then the labels follow each sprite's rotation/reflection.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88e364bc\" lines=\"102-115\">\n<description>\nSlide yellow pixels inside walled regions in the direction dictated by border-legend arrows.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color; yellow pixels sit inside enclosed regions bounded by colored walls.\n- Border legends: small rectangles on the grid edge whose fill color C matches a region's border color. Each legend contains a center dot and an indicator dot encoding a direction.\n\nRule:\n- For each enclosed region, identify its border color C and look up the legend with fill color C.\n- The direction from the legend's center dot to its indicator dot gives the slide direction.\n- All yellow pixels in that region slide in that direction, stacking against the far wall.\n\nKey insight: Legend fill color links to region border color; the center→indicator offset encodes the movement vector.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"9ba4a9aa\" lines=\"75-90\">\n<description>\nReturn the ring object reached by following the guide path from the checkerboard object.\n\nInput structure\n- The input has one dominant background color, one 3x3 checkerboard object,\n  several 3x3 ring objects, and sparse guide pixels on a two-cell lattice.\n\nRule\n- Find the checkerboard object and its unique adjacent guide pixel.\n- Follow guide pixels of that color through two-cell horizontal/vertical steps.\n- Output the unique 3x3 ring object adjacent to that connected guide path.\n\nKey insight\n- The checkerboard object is the source, and the color of its attached guide\n  pixel selects the path whose far endpoint points at the answer ring.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"88bcf3b4\" lines=\"229-241\">\n<description>\nSwing an irregular line segment around a nearby obstacle sprite, preserving its pixel count.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg, bar color (straight lines perpendicular to the grid edge, one end on the border), segment color (irregular stroke attached to bar's inner endpoint), obstacle color (sprite near the bar terminal).\n\nRule:\n- Each bar is perpendicular to the edge it touches; its inner (terminal) endpoint has an attached irregular segment.\n- The segment detaches and re-routes: it travels from the terminal toward the nearest obstacle, hugs the obstacle's boundary, then exits at a 45° diagonal.\n- Total painted pixels equal the original segment length.\n\nKey insight: The segment behaves like a rope swung around the obstacle — attach at the nearest point, trace the skin, exit diagonally, conserving length.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"412b6263\" lines=\"39-54\">\n<description>\nInfer the background, rotate the whole input 90 degrees counterclockwise, and stack two framed copies vertically.\n\nInput structure:\n- Blue is absent from the input.\n- One rare marker color appears in exactly two pixels.\n- The background is the unique non-marker color whose removal leaves exactly two 4-connected foreground objects, each with one marker pixel and one body color.\n- The whole grid, including background and foreground objects, is the sprite to transform.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
